"Defines a focused experiment by linking a testable hypothesis to a method, observable measure and advance success criterion.\n\nUse when: Design a business experiment before gathering evidence. Make the team's interpretation criteria inspectable and difficult to move afterward.\n\nAvoid when: The question requires an entire research program or a claim of full validation from one small test.\n\nVisual grammar: Four linked regions state hypothesis, test, measure and success threshold. Identifiers connect the card to assumptions and later findings. Any time, population and ethical constraints remain visible.\n\nBuild: Write one precise claim and identify the relevant target population. Choose a method capable of producing evidence about that claim. Declare the observation and threshold before running the experiment.\n\nCheck: The method actually tests the stated assumption. Success criteria are observable and specified in advance. Conclusions remain bounded by the test population and method.\n\nPitfalls: Choosing a convenient metric unrelated to the hypothesis. Changing the pass threshold after seeing the result.\n\nExample: A workshop provider tests whether local employers will reserve trial places, using a defined invitation and a predeclared reservation threshold.\n\nAlternatives: Assumption map, Lean UX Canvas."
